ed by I'm sorry t3 is the  first peak our t3 is in the middle and  then t4 is the last paint to come off so  baseline resolution great specificity  but again even though we know that this  is t3 our t3 and t4 the icp-ms is purely  just looking at iodine so again iodine  is really sort of the metal or the  element that we're actually measuring in  this situation to give us the  specificity for iodine so that's a  performance objective so I'll spend it's  just a couple of slides here at the end  kind of showing you where this method is  this isn't a method validation  presentation so I'm not going to show  all the details there but I will kind of  give you the idea that overall the  sensitivity is what sort of sidelined  this a little bit not to mention my time  winding down at ATP and not and no  longer having the ability to kind of  continue to work on the project but we  were about three-fold less sensitive  than what we really wanted to be even  using sort of a dilute and shoot as well  as a concentration step and I think a  lot of that had to do with the order ICP  that we were using and we had some ideas  as to  we might be able to boost that  sensitivity one of the really important  experiments I think we needed to do and  I was very happy with the outcome is we  were able to put a very high  concentration of t3 and we were able to  put a very high concentration of t4 so  this is about a hundred times higher  than what the AMR the upper end of the  AMR was and then we were able to put in  a normal amount of our t3 so about in  the middle of the AMR for our t3 and  this experiment is important because as  everybody knows when you get very high  concentrations of compounds you can  start having tailing with a tandem mass  spectrometer if we have our samples well  resolved by mass we might not have to  worry about that but this could be a  major issue for an icp-ms where again  we're only looking at iodine and so this  gives us a lot of confidence that with  the c